  "independent_mathematical_findings": [
    "MGF: threshold formulas give measurable exponentials for positive, negative and zero lambda. |Y| integrability is established by its nonnegative-integral measure and null complement; p belongs to [0,1]. The exponential is bounded above almost surely by exp(|lambda|), hence integrable by the same F9 argument; the affine chord majorant is integrable by F1 linearity. Taking expectations is therefore legitimate despite that elementary bound remaining implicit.",
    "MGF: g=1-p+p exp(lambda)>0 including p=0,1; q belongs to [0,1]; direct differentiation gives psi(0)=psi prime(0)=0 and psi double-prime=q(1-q)<=1/4. The positive-lambda kernel is lambda-s; the negative-lambda kernel is s-lambda on [lambda,0], each nonnegative with integral lambda squared/2. The endpoint formula follows from FTC by differentiating (lambda-s)psi prime(s)+psi(s); no Fubini or integration-by-parts assumption is needed. Exponentiating gives lambda squared/8.",
    "Uniform deviation: finite transformed independence, common-law expectation, scalar MGF and finite factorization yield exp(n lambda squared/8). Markov with lambda=4 epsilon and lambda=-4 epsilon gives both nonstrict tails exp(-2n epsilon squared); two finite union bounds yield 2|F| exp(-2n epsilon squared). The finite maximum is measurable by the declared arithmetic/lattice supplier. Finite nonempty class is essential for event equality.",
    "Choice: no selection across an arbitrary family, no continuous-map measurability supplier with Countable Choice hypothesis, and no AC Hoeffding sum theorem is invoked. Actual arithmetic/threshold/composition suppliers are choice-free at the used clauses; dyadic simple approximants are explicit and finite product factorization uses finitely many given probability laws. No extra axiom enters this finite scalar route."
  ],
